WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.875
JavaScript input validation requires explicit runtime checks.

00:00:03.875 --> 00:00:11.854
Browser controls, TypeScript types, and framework conventions are helpful, but they are not enough by themselves.

00:00:11.854 --> 00:00:24.847
Values from users, browsers, APIs, files, storage, messages, queues, third-party services, and environment variables should be treated as untrusted until the application has parsed and validated them.

00:00:24.847 --> 00:00:28.267
Strong validation starts with expected data.

00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:38.069
Define type, length, format, range, required fields, optional fields, allowed values, nested objects, arrays, and business rules before processing data.

00:00:38.069 --> 00:00:42.400
Be careful with dynamic typing and type coercion.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:48.099
Prefer clear checks and schemas over loose comparisons and ambiguous assumptions.

00:00:48.099 --> 00:00:51.974
Schema libraries can make validation more consistent.

00:00:51.974 --> 00:01:01.320
Zod, Joi, Yup, Ajv, and JSON Schema can define contracts and enforce them at runtime, especially at API boundaries.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:07.475
Client-side validation improves user experience, but server-side validation is the security requirement.

00:01:07.475 --> 00:01:14.086
Shared schemas can help reduce drift, but the server remains responsible for enforcement.

00:01:14.086 --> 00:01:18.873
The goal is predictable behavior and reduced security risk.

00:01:18.873 --> 00:01:29.587
Validate by context, pair validation with output encoding and safe APIs, reject fields users should not control, and test failure cases thoroughly.

00:01:29.587 --> 00:01:38.021
JavaScript applications are safer when invalid input is expected, handled deliberately, and prevented from reaching sensitive logic.
